1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.957
Analysts are paid to doubt confident numbers.

2
00:00:03.958 --> 00:00:17.535
So when an LLM — the kind of AI that reads documents and writes like a person — was pointed at deal documents for private-equity investors, the danger wasn't that it would be wrong.

3
00:00:17.536 --> 00:00:20.281
It was that it would be fluent.

4
00:00:21.382 --> 00:00:24.879
It refused to launch as a naked score.

5
00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:34.286
From day one, every claim carried its source — and thin evidence drew an honest, I'm not sure, instead of a guess.

6
00:00:34.287 --> 00:00:36.432
A score is a claim.

7
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:41.369
Experts don't buy claims — they buy claims with references.

8
00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:46.041
No score travels alone: the drawer is the proof surface.

9
00:00:46.042 --> 00:00:48.162
Explain this score.

10
00:00:48.163 --> 00:00:59.400
And the number opens into its evidence: the board pack, the audited statements — and the one claim that couldn't be verified, kept visible instead of hidden.

11
00:01:00.526 --> 00:01:11.805
Deal screening ran sixty percent faster, measured before and after rollout — because analysts stopped re-verifying every machine claim by hand.

12
00:01:11.806 --> 00:01:19.526
Product Manager and Designer on a cross-functional team — engineers, data scientists, a PM.

13
00:01:19.527 --> 00:01:28.862
The client is under NDA, so these are labelled reconstructions; the real artifacts are walked through on a call.

14
00:01:29.974 --> 00:01:42.730
Where this breaks: provenance-first — every claim beside its source — pays only when readers actually open the references, and wrong answers are expensive.

15
00:01:42.731 --> 00:01:46.452
Citations can't save a guessing model.

16
00:01:46.453 --> 00:01:50.848
For expert skeptics, provenance is the product.

17
00:01:50.849 --> 00:01:53.376
The full case is below.
